Found in the k119 review, pre-existing. On Windows, unreal.editor set to an absolute path without the .exe extension (e.g. C:/UE/Engine/Binaries/Win64/UnrealEditor) logs "path not found", although exec.Command resolves it via PATHEXT. Fix: in the existence check in cmd/launcher/helpers.go, on Windows also try the path with the PATHEXT extensions before warning, matching what os/exec does (exec.LookPath accepts a path with separators and applies PATHEXT). Done when the warning matches exec.Command on both platforms and a Windows-only test pins it (compile-checked; behaviour unverified until the Windows box).

Fix in cmd/launcher/helpers.go: new pathFound() behind the path branch of warnIfMissing. On Windows it uses exec.LookPath(path), which for a path with separators runs the same lookPathExts/findExecutable exec.Command uses (PATHEXT appended when no extension, no PATH search). Elsewhere it keeps fileExists (os.Stat), deliberately: LookPath on Unix also checks the execute bit and would print 'path not found' for an existing non-executable file, so Linux behaviour stays byte-for-byte. Tests in helpers_test.go: TestWarnIfMissingAppliesPathExtOnWindows (skips off Windows; compile-checked via GOOS=windows go vet and go test -c, NOT run: behaviour unverified until the Windows box) and TestWarnIfMissingAddsNoExtensionElsewhere (Linux: path without .exe next to UnrealEditor.exe still warns, exact message; green before and after; a hand-rolled path+'.exe' mutant turns it red). Side effect on Windows only: a directory as unreal.editor now warns (LookPath rejects directories), which matches exec. Drift for the unreagent board (not in scope): on Linux an existing but non-executable unreal.editor path is not warned although exec fails with permission denied; dev-box only, low value.

